  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA closed upper low had propagated to northern AR per the 07Z water \nvapor imagery. Further upstream a shortwave was noted moving across \nthe northern plains with another upper low over the Gulf of Alaska. \nSurface obs continued to show a ridge axis across central KS with the\ncenter of the high pressure over the Great Lakes. \n\nFor Thanksgiving models continue to show little if any vertical \nmotion for precip with a stable airmass over the forecast area. So \nthe main impact from the upper low looks to be some increase in mid \nand high clouds. This combined with continued northerly surface \nwinds should hold highs in the lower 40s. Parts of north central KS \nmay see highs warm into the middle 40s with better insolation. \n\nThe surface ridge finally breaks down on Friday allowing a south and \nwesterly low level flow to develop. This is forecast to advect warm \nair into the region with highs generally in the middle 50s for both \nFriday and Saturday. Models are in good agreement now with the next \nshortwave moving through the central plains Saturday night. And \nthere is a consensus for good forcing and adequate moisture with \nthis wave. The general idea is for much of the precip to be driven \nby mid level frontogenesis on the north and northwest side of the \nsurface wave. The NBM has increased POPs into the 70 to 85 percent \nrange and this fits well with the ensembles. The main question with \nthis system is whether there will be some snow mix in on the back \nside of the precipitation. The system looks to remain warm enough \nfor all rain as it begins late in the afternoon Saturday and \nSaturday evening. But models continue to show good cold air \nadvection on the back side of the wave. Forecast soundings show the \ncolumn cooling to below freezing for all but the lowest 1500 feet or \nso. And the deterministic 00Z runs have some snow accumulations \novernight as the temps drop. But the NBM shows the probability for \nsnow as the precip type around 10 percent or less while the 00Z \nensembles put the chance for snow accumulations around 5 to 10 \npercent. The pattern favors the idea of some light snow mixing in on \nthe trailing edge of the precip shield with little or no impacts. \nbased on the thermal progs, the deterministic snow accumulations \nlook to be to aggressive. The forecast sticks with the more probable \noutcome of an all rain event. But I wouldn't be surprised if a light \ndusting of snow occurred along the NEB state line Sunday morning. \n\nAnother low amplitude shortwave is progged to move across the \ncentral plains early next week. Models continue to fail to generate \nQPF with this wave as surface ridging along the TX gulf coast \ninitially hampers moisture return. Meanwhile the northern branch of \nthe jet stream is progged to remain across southern Canada which \nshould allow for a southerly component to the low level flow and \nmoderation in temps for the first half of next week.",
